<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beaujeu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beaujeu is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the hills west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>. Its castle was the seat of the lords of Beaujeu from the 10th century, who gave their name to the surrounding Beaujolais and later founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villefranche-sur-saone">Villefranche-sur-Saône</a>. The lordship passed to the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> in 1400, and the town remains a centre of the Beaujolais wine trade.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They cultivated close ties with the French crown and with the lords of <b>Beaujeu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-forez">County of Forez</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of <b>Beaujeu</b> appear in the 10th century as castellans in the hills west of the Saône, between the counts of Mâcon and Forez and the archbishops of Lyon.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-beaujeu">Lordship of Beaujeu</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dombes, a plateau of ponds east of the Saône, belonged to the lords of <b>Beaujeu</b> and then the dukes of Bourbon.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-dombes">Principality of Dombes</a></li></ul></section>
